---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="DevSecOps Deployment Tracker — Case Study | Ismail Triki"
  description="Deep-dive into a software-supply-chain-security pipeline: Jenkins, Trivy, Syft SBOM, Cosign signing and attestation, and a hardened Kubernetes deployment for a Flask API."
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main-content" class="pt-32 pb-24 max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">

    <!-- Back -->
    <a href={base} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs text-text-muted hover:text-accent-cyan transition-colors mb-12 group">
      <svg class="group-hover:-translate-x-1 transition-transform" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
        <line x1="19" y1="12" x2="5" y2="12"/>
        <polyline points="12 19 5 12 12 5"/>
      </svg>
      Back to portfolio
    </a>

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="mb-16">
      <p class="section-label mb-3">Case Study</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl text-text-primary leading-tight mb-4">
        DevSecOps Deployment Tracker
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-text-muted mb-6">
        Signed, Attested Container Pipeline for a Flask API on Kubernetes
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {['Jenkins', 'Trivy', 'Gitleaks', 'Syft / SBOM', 'Cosign', 'GHCR', 'Kubernetes', 'Flask', 'Docker'].map((t) => (
          <span class="tag">{t}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content sections -->
    <div class="space-y-16 prose-custom">

      <!-- 1. Problem / objective -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Problem / Objective</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">Beyond "tests pass"</h2>
        <div class="text-text-muted space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Most CI pipelines run lint and tests, then stop. The question behind this project was different:
            what does it take to go from "the code passes tests" to "we can prove exactly what's running,
            that its dependencies are clean, and that the image hasn't been tampered with between build and
            deployment" — the kind of software-supply-chain assurance that incidents like SolarWinds and
            Log4Shell made a standard expectation rather than a nice-to-have.
          </p>
          <p>
            The objective was to build that full chain — from a commit to a cryptographically signed, attested,
            digest-pinned container running on Kubernetes — around a deliberately small Flask application, so
            the pipeline itself is the thing being demonstrated, not the app it wraps.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 2. Architecture -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Architecture</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">From commit to running workload</h2>
        <div class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">
          <p>
            Every stage runs on a self-hosted Jenkins agent, not a GitHub-hosted runner. The pipeline is
            gate-based: a failure at a security stage stops it before an unscanned or unsigned image can reach
            the registry or the cluster. The exact stage order and what's blocking vs. advisory is detailed in
            the CI/CD Pipeline and Security Gates sections below — this diagram gives the system-level view.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Architecture diagram -->
        <figure class="mb-4">
          <a
            href={`${base}/images/projects/devsecops/devsecops-pipeline-architecture.png`}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="block group"
            aria-label="Open full-size DevSecOps pipeline architecture diagram in new tab"
          >
            <img
              src={`${base}/images/projects/devsecops/devsecops-pipeline-architecture.png`}
              alt="End-to-end DevSecOps pipeline architecture diagram showing source and development, Jenkins CI orchestration with 13 pipeline steps, security and quality gates, software supply-chain artifacts (GHCR, immutable digest, SBOM, Cosign signature and attestation), Kubernetes deployment target, and evidence and reports."
              loading="lazy"
              class="w-full max-w-3xl mx-auto rounded-xl border border-bg-600 shadow-xl shadow-black/50
                     transition-all duration-300
                     group-hover:border-accent-cyan/30 group-hover:shadow-accent-cyan/5"
              onerror="this.style.display='none'; this.nextElementSibling.style.display='flex';"
            />
            <div
              style="display:none;"
              class="w-full max-w-3xl mx-auto rounded-xl border border-bg-600 bg-bg-700 flex items-center justify-center py-16"
            >
              <p class="font-mono text-sm text-text-dim">Architecture diagram coming soon.</p>
            </div>
          </a>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-dim text-center leading-relaxed">
            End-to-end DevSecOps architecture showing CI/CD orchestration, security gates,
            software-supply-chain artifacts, and hardened Kubernetes deployment.
            <a
              href={`${base}/images/projects/devsecops/devsecops-pipeline-architecture.png`}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="ml-1 font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan/60 hover:text-accent-cyan transition-colors whitespace-nowrap"
            >
              Open full size →
            </a>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-text-dim text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          This diagram is a system-level illustration, not a byte-for-byte export of the Jenkinsfile — the
          numbered stage list and the specific gate behavior in the sections below are the audited source of
          truth.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 3. CI/CD pipeline -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">CI/CD Pipeline</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">12 stages, one Jenkinsfile</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-bg-700">
                <th class="text-left font-mono text-xs text-text-dim uppercase tracking-widest py-3 pr-8">#</th>
                <th class="text-left font-mono text-xs text-text-dim uppercase tracking-widest py-3 pr-8">Stage</th>
                <th class="text-left font-mono text-xs text-text-dim uppercase tracking-widest py-3">What it does</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-text-muted">
              {[
                ['1', 'Gitleaks', 'Scans the full git history for committed secrets before anything else runs'],
                ['2', 'Test setup', 'Builds the Python venv and installs dependencies'],
                ['3', 'pytest', 'Runs the automated test suite'],
                ['4', 'Ruff', 'Lints the codebase'],
                ['5', 'Bandit', 'Static application security testing (SAST)'],
                ['6', 'pip-audit', 'Scans dependencies for known vulnerabilities'],
                ['7', 'Hadolint', 'Lints the Dockerfile (advisory, non-blocking)'],
                ['8', 'Docker build', 'Multi-stage image build via Buildx, with provenance'],
                ['9', 'Trivy', 'Scans the built image; blocks the build on fixable HIGH/CRITICAL findings'],
                ['10', 'Syft', 'Generates a CycloneDX SBOM from the built image'],
                ['11', 'GHCR publish', 'Pushes the image to a private registry and captures its digest'],
                ['12', 'Cosign', 'Signs the image, verifies the signature, then attests and verifies the SBOM and SLSA provenance'],
              ].map(([n, stage, desc]) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-bg-800 hover:bg-bg-700/30 transition-colors">
                  <td class="py-3 pr-8 font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan">{n}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 pr-8 text-text-primary font-medium">{stage}</td>
                  <td class="py-3">{desc}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 4. Security gates -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Security Gates</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">What actually blocks the pipeline</h2>
        <div class="text-text-muted space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Not every check is a hard gate, and being precise about which ones are is part of the point of this
            project. <strong class="text-text-primary">Gitleaks</strong> and the pytest/Ruff/Bandit/pip-audit
            stages fail the build on error by default. <strong class="text-text-primary">Trivy</strong> is
            explicitly configured to fail the build (<code class="font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan bg-bg-700 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">--exit-code 1</code>)
            on fixable HIGH/CRITICAL vulnerabilities. <strong class="text-text-primary">Hadolint</strong> currently
            runs as an advisory step only — its findings are visible in the logs but don't stop the pipeline.
            GHCR publish failures are explicit and fail the build rather than being silently swallowed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 5. Container and supply-chain security -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Container & Supply-Chain Security</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">From a built image to a trusted one</h2>
        <div class="text-text-muted space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            The Dockerfile builds a non-root runtime image with a healthcheck. Trivy's image scan initially
            flagged real HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs in Debian base-image packages; the fix was pinning specific package
            versions in the Dockerfile rather than suppressing the findings, and the scan now reports zero
            vulnerabilities against the pinned image.
          </p>
          <p>
            The built image is published to a private GHCR registry — the Kubernetes manifests reference it via
            an <code class="font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan bg-bg-700 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">imagePullSecret</code>,
            consistent with private (not public) registry access — and is always referenced downstream by its
            SHA256 digest, never by a mutable tag.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 6. SBOM, signing, attestations -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">SBOM, Signing &amp; Attestations</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">Proving what's running, not just scanning it</h2>
        <div class="text-text-muted space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Syft generates a CycloneDX-format SBOM directly from the built image. Cosign then signs the image
            using a key pair (key-based signing, not keyless/OIDC) and verifies that signature in the same
            pipeline run.
          </p>
          <p>
            The SBOM and the build's SLSA provenance are both attached to the image as
            <strong class="text-text-primary"> in-toto attestations</strong> via <code class="font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan bg-bg-700 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">cosign attest</code> —
            not just uploaded as separate pipeline artifacts — and each is verified with
            <code class="font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan bg-bg-700 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">cosign verify-attestation</code> before
            the pipeline completes. That distinction matters: an attestation is cryptographically bound to the
            image and can be independently re-verified later; a plain artifact upload is not.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 7. Kubernetes deployment and runtime hardening -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Kubernetes Deployment</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">Runtime hardening, checked field by field</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-bg-700">
                <th class="text-left font-mono text-xs text-text-dim uppercase tracking-widest py-3 pr-8">Control</th>
                <th class="text-left font-mono text-xs text-text-dim uppercase tracking-widest py-3">Configuration</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-text-muted">
              {[
                ['Image reference', 'By SHA256 digest, not tag'],
                ['Replicas', '2'],
                ['Run as non-root', 'runAsNonRoot: true, runAsUser/Group: 10001'],
                ['Seccomp profile', 'RuntimeDefault'],
                ['Privilege escalation', 'allowPrivilegeEscalation: false'],
                ['Linux capabilities', 'drop: [ALL]'],
                ['Probes', 'readinessProbe + livenessProbe on /health'],
                ['Service', 'ClusterIP'],
                ['NetworkPolicy', 'Ingress scoped to the app\'s own pod selector'],
              ].map(([control, config]) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-bg-800 hover:bg-bg-700/30 transition-colors">
                  <td class="py-3 pr-8 text-text-primary font-medium">{control}</td>
                  <td class="py-3 font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan">{config}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-text-dim">
          Honest gap: readOnlyRootFilesystem is not currently set on the container — noted here rather than
          implied as done.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 8. Evidence / validation -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Evidence / Validation</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">What the pipeline actually produced</h2>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          {[
            { label: 'Gitleaks', result: 'Clean — no secrets found' },
            { label: 'pytest', result: '6/6 tests passing' },
            { label: 'Trivy image scan', result: '0 vulnerabilities, after Debian package-pinning fixes' },
            { label: 'SBOM', result: 'Valid CycloneDX document, thousands of components catalogued' },
            { label: 'Cosign verify', result: 'Signature verification succeeds against the published digest' },
            { label: 'Cosign verify-attestation', result: 'SBOM and SLSA provenance attestations both verify' },
          ].map((item) => (
            <div class="card-base border border-bg-600">
              <p class="font-mono text-xs text-accent-cyan uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">{item.label}</p>
              <p class="text-text-muted text-sm">{item.result}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="mt-5 font-mono text-xs text-text-dim">
          These results are captured as Jenkins build artifacts on each pipeline run — they aren't committed
          into the git repository itself, so reproducing them means running the pipeline, not just reading the
          repo.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 9. Key engineering decisions -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Key Engineering Decisions</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-6">Choices worth calling out</h2>
        <div class="space-y-5">
          {[
            {
              title: 'Trivy blocks, Hadolint doesn\'t',
              detail: 'Vulnerability exposure is treated as a hard gate; Dockerfile style/best-practice issues are visible but advisory. Not every finding deserves the same severity of response.',
            },
            {
              title: 'Digest everywhere downstream',
              detail: 'The image is referenced by SHA256 digest in every downstream config, never by a mutable tag — the Kubernetes Deployment is guaranteed to run the exact image that was scanned, signed, and attested.',
            },
            {
              title: 'Attestations, not just artifacts',
              detail: 'The SBOM and provenance are attached to the image as verifiable Cosign attestations, so they can be checked independently of the CI system later, not only trusted because "the pipeline said so."',
            },
          ].map((item) => (
            <div class="card-base border border-bg-600">
              <p class="text-text-primary font-medium text-sm mb-1">{item.title}</p>
              <p class="text-text-muted text-sm">{item.detail}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 10. Challenges and lessons learned -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Challenges &amp; Lessons Learned</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-4">What went wrong, and how it was fixed</h2>
        <div class="space-y-5">
          {[
            {
              problem: 'Trivy flagged real HIGH/CRITICAL CVEs in Debian base-image packages',
              solution: 'Pinned specific package versions in the Dockerfile instead of suppressing or ignoring the findings; re-scanned and confirmed zero vulnerabilities.',
            },
            {
              problem: 'GHCR push failures were not surfacing as pipeline failures',
              solution: 'Reworked the publish stage so a failed push explicitly fails the build, instead of the pipeline silently continuing with no image published.',
            },
            {
              problem: 'Dockerfile line-continuation syntax broke the multi-stage build',
              solution: 'Restored correct line continuations and stabilized the build stage.',
            },
            {
              problem: 'Transient fetch failures caused intermittent pipeline failures',
              solution: 'Iterated on the pipeline\'s reliability across several retry commits until fetch steps were stable.',
            },
          ].map((item) => (
            <div class="card-base border border-bg-600">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="text-red-400/60 mt-1 shrink-0">✕</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-text-muted text-sm mb-2"><span class="text-text-primary font-medium">Problem:</span> {item.problem}</p>
                  <p class="text-text-muted text-sm"><span class="text-accent-green font-medium">Fix:</span> {item.solution}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 11. Current status -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Current Status</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-6">Completed vs. in progress</h2>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div>
            <p class="font-mono text-xs text-accent-green uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">Completed</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              {[
                'Flask API + automated pytest suite',
                'Jenkins pipeline: Gitleaks, Ruff, Bandit, pip-audit, Hadolint',
                'Docker build with a blocking Trivy scan',
                'Syft SBOM generation (CycloneDX)',
                'Private GHCR publish by immutable digest',
                'Cosign image signing + verification',
                'Cosign SBOM + SLSA provenance attestation + verification',
                'Kubernetes Deployment, Service, and NetworkPolicy manifests, committed',
              ].map((item) => (
                <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm">
                  <span class="text-accent-green mt-1.5 shrink-0">✓</span>
                  <span class="text-text-muted">{item}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-mono text-xs text-text-dim uppercase tracking-widest mb-3">In progress / not yet committed</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2">
              {[
                'NetworkPolicy is written but not yet validated against a live cluster',
                'A Helm chart scaffold exists locally (Chart.yaml + values.yaml only, no templates) — not yet committed to the repository',
                'Scan/SBOM/attestation reports exist only as local Jenkins-build artifacts, not committed evidence in the repo',
                'CI currently runs on a self-hosted Jenkins agent only — no GitHub-hosted-runner-compatible path yet',
              ].map((item) => (
                <li class="flex items-start gap-3 text-sm">
                  <span class="text-text-dim mt-1.5 shrink-0">○</span>
                  <span class="text-text-muted">{item}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="border-t border-bg-700"></div>

      <!-- 12. Next steps -->
      <section>
        <p class="section-label">Next Steps</p>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl text-text-primary mb-6">What's next</h2>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          {[
            'Finish and commit the Helm chart, replacing the raw manifests with templated, versioned releases',
            'Validate the NetworkPolicy against a running cluster (apply it, confirm allowed/denied traffic behaves as expected)',
            'Commit scan/SBOM/attestation evidence into the repository so it\'s reviewable without re-running the pipeline',
            'Add a portable CI path that doesn\'t depend on a self-hosted runner',
          ].map((point) => (
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3 card-base border border-bg-600">
              <span class="text-accent-cyan mt-0.5 shrink-0">▸</span>
              <p class="text-text-muted text-sm">{point}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>

    <!-- Repo link -->
    <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-bg-700 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
      <a
        href="https://github.com/iiismailtriki/devsecops-deployment-tracker"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="btn-primary text-sm"
      >
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor">
          <path d="M12 0C5.37 0 0 5.37 0 12c0 5.3 3.44 9.8 8.21 11.39.6.11.82-.26.82-.58v-2.03c-3.34.73-4.04-1.61-4.04-1.61-.55-1.39-1.34-1.76-1.34-1.76-1.09-.74.08-.73.08-.73 1.21.09 1.84 1.24 1.84 1.24 1.07 1.84 2.81 1.3 3.5.99.11-.78.42-1.3.76-1.6-2.67-.3-5.47-1.33-5.47-5.93 0-1.31.47-2.38 1.24-3.22-.13-.3-.54-1.52.12-3.18 0 0 1.01-.32 3.3 1.23a11.5 11.5 0 0 1 3-.4c1.02.005 2.04.138 3 .4 2.28-1.55 3.29-1.23 3.29-1.23.66 1.66.25 2.88.12 3.18.77.84 1.24 1.91 1.24 3.22 0 4.61-2.81 5.63-5.48 5.92.43.37.81 1.1.81 2.22v3.29c0 .32.21.7.82.58C20.56 21.8 24 17.3 24 12c0-6.63-5.37-12-12-12z"/>
        </svg>
        View repository
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- Back link -->
    <div class="mt-8 pt-8 border-t border-bg-700">
      <a href={base} class="btn-ghost">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
          <line x1="19" y1="12" x2="5" y2="12"/>
          <polyline points="12 19 5 12 12 5"/>
        </svg>
        Back to portfolio
      </a>
    </div>

  </main>
</Base>
